WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:11.650
Every carbon atom in your body got here by climbing a rung inside a star that looked impossible to climb: what comes out when two helium nuclei join lives for about a quadrillionth of a second and falls apart.

2
00:00:11.650 --> 00:00:18.500
So for carbon to form, a third nucleus had to arrive in exactly that gap before anything had come apart.

3
00:00:18.500 --> 00:00:28.710
In nineteen fifty-three an astronomer said that for this to be possible there had to exist, in nature, an energy level no one had yet seen — and he said where it was, too.

4
00:00:36.910 --> 00:00:42.640
At the center of a star, when the hydrogen runs out, a strange silence settles in.

5
00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:52.530
For billions of years, millions of tons of hydrogen have been turning into helium in that core every second, and the energy pressing outward has held the star's own weight at bay.

6
00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.700
When the fuel is spent, the balance breaks.

7
00:00:55.700 --> 00:01:02.070
The core collapses onto itself; as it collapses it compresses, and as it compresses it heats.

8
00:01:02.070 --> 00:01:06.040
The temperature climbs to 100 million degrees.

9
00:01:06.040 --> 00:01:09.530
There is no hydrogen left now.

10
00:01:09.530 --> 00:01:13.820
What remains is the helium ash the star made for itself.

11
00:01:13.820 --> 00:01:21.950
And it is precisely here that the most ordinary-looking question in the universe arises: can anything heavier be built out of this ash?

12
00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:27.520
The ladder of the elements looks, at first glance, like an easy ladder to climb.

13
00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.610
You add a proton or a neutron to a nucleus and you step up one rung.

14
00:01:32.610 --> 00:01:39.140
From hydrogen to helium, from helium to lithium, then to beryllium, to boron, to carbon.

15
00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:43.110
On paper, a flawless sequence of steps.

16
00:01:43.110 --> 00:01:46.600
But nature never built two of this ladder's rungs.

17
00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:49.290
The first is rung number 5.

18
00:01:49.290 --> 00:01:58.220
A helium-4 nucleus is an extraordinarily stable, tightly packed lump of four particles; that is why it is given a name of its own and called an alpha particle.

19
00:01:58.220 --> 00:02:01.710
Add a proton to it and you would have lithium-5.

20
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:04.720
Add a neutron and you would have helium-5.

21
00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:07.250
Neither of them exists.

22
00:02:07.250 --> 00:02:11.700
More precisely, they barely even attempt to exist: once formed,

23
00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:21.750
within about a billionth of a trillionth of a second — a span as short as the time light needs to cross an atomic nucleus from one side to the other — they spit the extra particle back out.

24
00:02:21.750 --> 00:02:25.560
There is no stable nucleus with mass number 5.

25
00:02:25.560 --> 00:02:28.570
On the ladder, there is no rung.

26
00:02:28.570 --> 00:02:32.060
So you could skip 5 and go straight to 8.

27
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:37.470
Smash two alpha particles together; eight particles in total, beryllium-8.

28
00:02:37.470 --> 00:02:40.160
This nucleus really does form.

29
00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:46.530
It has been measured in the laboratory, its energy is known, its identity is beyond dispute.

30
00:02:46.530 --> 00:02:56.260
It has only one flaw: it prefers to sit as two separate alpha particles rather than as a single combined nucleus, by about 92 kiloelectronvolts.

31
00:02:56.260 --> 00:03:01.190
Beryllium-8, in other words, is less stable than the two pieces that make it.

32
00:03:01.190 --> 00:03:03.240
The outcome is inevitable.

33
00:03:03.240 --> 00:03:05.930
It forms and it falls apart.

34
00:03:05.930 --> 00:03:10.380
Its lifetime is roughly one ten-quadrillionth of a second.

35
00:03:10.380 --> 00:03:13.710
There is no way to picture a span that short.

36
00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:23.280
The only thing one can say is this: in the time it takes you to hear this sentence, trillions upon trillions of lifetimes have come and gone for a beryllium-8 nucleus.

37
00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.130
Rung number 8 is missing as well.

38
00:03:26.130 --> 00:03:28.980
And that means the ladder is severed outright.

39
00:03:28.980 --> 00:03:37.110
Because in a stellar core filled with helium ash there are no protons left over; the route upward by adding one particle at a time is closed.

40
00:03:37.110 --> 00:03:43.800
If all you hold in your hand are alpha particles, then joining two of them brings you to 8, and 8 does not hold.

41
00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.250
This same break raised the same wall in the first moments of the universe.

42
00:03:48.250 --> 00:03:58.940
In the first few minutes after the Big Bang, the temperature and density were right for nuclear reactions; the universe made hydrogen, helium, and a trace of lithium.

43
00:03:58.940 --> 00:04:04.670
Then expansion pulled everything apart, the temperature fell, and the window closed.

44
00:04:04.670 --> 00:04:08.640
There was not enough time to cross the gap between 5 and 8.

45
00:04:08.640 --> 00:04:13.090
The cosmic kitchen shut down before the cooking had properly begun.

46
00:04:13.090 --> 00:04:19.780
The conclusion that follows, viewed coldly, is this: carbon should not exist.

47
00:04:19.780 --> 00:04:22.470
Oxygen should not exist.

48
00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:27.720
Iron, calcium, phosphorus, none of them should exist.

49
00:04:27.720 --> 00:04:32.970
The universe should have been a place made of hydrogen and helium, staying plain forever.

50
00:04:32.970 --> 00:04:42.540
Stars could have flared and faded, but nothing would ever have been left behind to build a planet, no silicate to build rock, no carbon skeleton to build a molecule.

51
00:04:42.540 --> 00:04:50.510
And yet carbon is the fourth most abundant element in the universe by mass, after hydrogen, helium, and oxygen.

52
00:04:50.510 --> 00:04:54.320
It is everywhere we turn our eyes.

53
00:04:54.320 --> 00:05:02.770
In the spectra of stars, in interstellar clouds, in the ice of comets, in ink, in coal, in the backbone of DNA.

54
00:05:02.770 --> 00:05:09.940
There is carbon in the cartilage of the ear hearing these words right now, and in the membranes of the nerve cells making sense of that sound.

55
00:05:09.940 --> 00:05:13.750
About 18 percent of your body's mass is carbon.

56
00:05:13.750 --> 00:05:18.360
So somewhere, somehow, something jumped across that broken rung.

57
00:05:18.360 --> 00:05:24.090
That is the puzzle: how was a bridge crossed that falls apart at 8?

58
00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:30.460
The first clue came from looking at beryllium-8's embarrassingly brief lifetime from an entirely different angle.

59
00:05:30.460 --> 00:05:42.910
In 1951 and 1952, two researchers working independently of one another — one the Estonian astronomer Ernst Öpik, the other Edwin Salpeter, working in America — noticed the same thing.

60
00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:49.440
One ten-quadrillionth of a second is nothing on a human scale; but not on the scale of an atomic nucleus.

61
00:05:49.440 --> 00:05:57.570
Compared with the time it takes two alpha particles to pass by each other, the lifetime of beryllium-8 is hundreds of thousands of times longer.

62
00:05:57.570 --> 00:06:01.220
For a nucleus, that is not a fleeting instant.

63
00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:03.750
That is ample time.

64
00:06:03.750 --> 00:06:08.520
Salpeter's transformative move was to stop seeing beryllium-8 as a product.

65
00:06:08.520 --> 00:06:12.650
It had to be thought of as an equilibrium population instead.

66
00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:18.540
At that temperature, in a stellar core full of helium, alpha particles collide ceaselessly.

67
00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:25.070
Every second an unimaginable number of beryllium-8 nuclei form, and every one of them promptly falls apart.

68
00:06:25.070 --> 00:06:33.840
But because the rates of formation and decay balance each other, at every instant there is a small but steady reservoir of beryllium-8 waiting inside the core.

69
00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.410
A dilution on the order of one part in a billion; so little as to be almost nothing.

70
00:06:39.410 --> 00:06:42.260
Yet almost nothing is not nothing.

71
00:06:42.260 --> 00:06:50.870
And before a beryllium-8 nucleus comes apart, within that tiny interval of time, a third alpha particle can arrive and strike it.

72
00:06:50.870 --> 00:06:54.040
Three alphas, twelve particles.

73
00:06:54.040 --> 00:06:56.730
Carbon-12.

74
00:06:56.730 --> 00:07:04.380
That is how the bridge was built: not in a single move, but by stepping onto a collapsing rung before the collapse was complete.

75
00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:08.030
The trouble was that the arithmetic did not support it.

76
00:07:08.030 --> 00:07:18.560
When Fred Hoyle put the rate of this reaction into stellar models in the early 1950s, the amount of carbon that came out was laughably small next to the amount of carbon observed in the universe.

77
00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.930
Something somewhere was missing.

78
00:07:20.930 --> 00:07:26.820
The reasoning Hoyle then carried out is one of the boldest backward inferences in the history of physics.

79
00:07:26.820 --> 00:07:33.670
He did not start from theory and then look at nature; he looked at nature and declared what theory was obliged to say.

80
00:07:33.670 --> 00:07:37.480
Carbon exists, he said, and in this quantity.

81
00:07:37.480 --> 00:07:43.370
Therefore the fusion of three alphas has to be many times faster than a straightforward calculation would predict.

82
00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:51.180
And in nuclear physics only one mechanism is known that can speed up a reaction like that: resonance.

83
00:07:51.180 --> 00:07:54.990
To understand resonance, think of a swing.

84
00:07:54.990 --> 00:08:04.080
Push the swing at random moments and it hardly rises; push it at the moments that match its own natural rhythm and, with the very same force, it climbs far higher.

85
00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:09.010
Atomic nuclei, likewise, can exist only at certain discrete energy steps.

86
00:08:09.010 --> 00:08:18.900
If the energy the colliding particles bring with them happens to land on one of the target nucleus's permitted steps, the probability of fusion rises by hundreds or thousands of times.

87
00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:23.990
If it does not land there, the colliding partners simply scatter apart.

88
00:08:23.990 --> 00:08:29.400
Hoyle saw that he could calculate the required energy, because all the inputs were known.

89
00:08:29.400 --> 00:08:39.290
The sum of the masses of a beryllium-8 nucleus and an alpha particle lies 7.367 megaelectronvolts above the most stable state of carbon-12.

90
00:08:39.290 --> 00:08:44.380
The thermal motion in a stellar core adds a few hundred kiloelectronvolts more.

91
00:08:44.380 --> 00:08:52.350
Therefore, Hoyle said, the carbon-12 nucleus must have an excited energy level at roughly 7.68 megaelectronvolts.

92
00:08:52.350 --> 00:08:59.360
What is more, the rotational properties of that level had to be of a particular kind, or the reaction still would not work.

93
00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:04.610
Among the known energy levels of carbon-12 in those years, no such thing existed.

94
00:09:04.610 --> 00:09:15.940
In 1953, Hoyle went to the California Institute of Technology and told the experimenters doing nuclear measurements there, Ward Whaling in particular, that they needed to go looking for this level.

95
00:09:15.940 --> 00:09:26.630
The request was extraordinary: an astronomer, on the grounds that carbon exists in the universe, was asking a nuclear physicist to find an energy step that no one had ever seen.

96
00:09:26.630 --> 00:09:33.640
Whaling and his colleagues Dunbar, Pixley, and Wenzel set up the experiment at the Kellogg Radiation Laboratory.

97
00:09:33.640 --> 00:09:41.610
They bombarded nitrogen-14 nuclei with deuterons; the products of that reaction are a carbon-12 nucleus and an alpha particle.

98
00:09:41.610 --> 00:09:50.540
By measuring carefully the energies of the emerging alpha particles, it was possible to read off which energy levels the carbon left behind had been placed in.

99
00:09:50.540 --> 00:09:52.590
The level was there.

100
00:09:52.590 --> 00:10:02.480
The result, published that same year, reported an excited state at 7.68 megaelectronvolts with an uncertainty of 0.03 megaelectronvolts.

101
00:10:02.480 --> 00:10:05.970
Where Hoyle wanted it, and as sharply as he wanted it.

102
00:10:05.970 --> 00:10:09.460
The story was completed a few years later.

103
00:10:09.460 --> 00:10:20.950
In 1957, Cook, Fowler, and the two Lauritsens showed that this level, when it decays, breaks into three alpha particles, and that it carries the rotational properties being sought.

104
00:10:20.950 --> 00:10:25.720
It was not merely in the right place; it was of the right kind.

105
00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:33.690
Today this energy step is called the Hoyle state, and modern measurements put it at 7.654 megaelectronvolts.

106
00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:36.700
With that, the bridge was complete.

107
00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:42.590
Two alphas take on a debt of 92 kiloelectronvolts for an instant and assemble beryllium-8.

108
00:10:42.590 --> 00:10:46.240
The third alpha arrives before the debt comes due.

109
00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:59.650
Carbon-12 is born in a trembling, strained excited state, and most of the time it falls back into three alphas; but once in thousands of attempts, it emits a gamma ray, sheds its excess energy,

110
00:10:59.650 --> 00:11:02.660
and settles into stable carbon.

111
00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:09.990
One carbon out of three heliums, releasing 7.275 megaelectronvolts of energy in total.

112
00:11:09.990 --> 00:11:15.720
Finding the Hoyle state closed one question and immediately opened a far more unsettling one.

113
00:11:15.720 --> 00:11:22.090
Because the position of that level was not merely right; it was placed with a strange and startling fineness.

114
00:11:22.090 --> 00:11:29.580
The energy that a beryllium-8 nucleus and an alpha particle bring between them is 7.367 megaelectronvolts.

115
00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.230
The Hoyle state sits at 7.654.

116
00:11:33.230 --> 00:11:36.880
The difference is 287 kiloelectronvolts.

117
00:11:36.880 --> 00:11:41.010
The thermal motion inside the star closes exactly that gap.

118
00:11:41.010 --> 00:11:50.740
The triple-alpha reaction is exponentially sensitive to the size of that difference: the probability that the particles can find that much extra energy varies sharply with temperature.

119
00:11:50.740 --> 00:11:59.190
Indeed, the energy production of helium burning depends on roughly the fortieth power of the temperature, one of the steepest dependences known.

120
00:11:59.190 --> 00:12:02.840
A difference of 10 degrees shifts the rate measurably.

121
00:12:02.840 --> 00:12:07.450
To see what this implies, you have to move the level in your imagination.

122
00:12:07.450 --> 00:12:18.140
Since the late 1980s more than one research group has done precisely that: shifting the position of the Hoyle state a few hundred kiloelectronvolts up or down and running stellar models again.

123
00:12:18.140 --> 00:12:23.230
The results, though the uncertainties are large, all point the same way.

124
00:12:23.230 --> 00:12:31.520
Move the level a few hundred kiloelectronvolts up, and the reaction slows so much that stars can barely convert helium into carbon at all.

125
00:12:31.520 --> 00:12:38.370
Move it down, and carbon is produced so easily that nearly all of it is swept on to the next step, oxygen.

126
00:12:38.370 --> 00:12:43.460
In either case the universe becomes a place where carbon-based chemistry cannot develop.

127
00:12:43.460 --> 00:12:53.030
The window being discussed is roughly 100 kiloelectronvolts wide; very small on the scale of particle physics, very large in its cosmic consequences.

128
00:12:53.030 --> 00:13:02.440
In 2013, lattice calculations starting from quark masses suggested that this window corresponds to changes of a few percent in the fundamental constants.

129
00:13:02.440 --> 00:13:05.130
Here one has to be very careful.

130
00:13:05.130 --> 00:13:14.060
These calculations are genuine physics results, and they teach us something: how sensitive the abundance of carbon is to the fine details of nuclear physics.

131
00:13:14.060 --> 00:13:18.830
But they say nothing whatsoever about why that sensitivity is what it is.

132
00:13:18.830 --> 00:13:27.760
To draw a purpose, a design, or an intention out of this belongs not to physics but to philosophy, and no agreed answer exists there either.

133
00:13:27.760 --> 00:13:35.730
The one solid observation we have is this: in a universe capable of making carbon, beings curious about carbon can also appear.

134
00:13:35.730 --> 00:13:42.900
Everything known about the fineness of the Hoyle state ends there; the rest is not yet science and should not be presented as such.

135
00:13:42.900 --> 00:13:48.150
The less familiar, and perhaps subtler, half of the story lies on the next rung.

136
00:13:48.150 --> 00:13:55.000
Once carbon-12 has been assembled, it can capture one more alpha particle and turn into oxygen-16.

137
00:13:55.000 --> 00:14:04.890
If this second reaction also had a resonance sitting in exactly the right place, stars would convert all the carbon they make into oxygen, and no carbon would be left.

138
00:14:04.890 --> 00:14:07.260
There is no such resonance.

139
00:14:07.260 --> 00:14:16.350
The energy levels of oxygen-16 fall outside the places that would accelerate this reaction, and so the conversion of carbon into oxygen limps along slowly,

140
00:14:16.350 --> 00:14:21.440
and when the helium is exhausted the star is left holding both carbon and oxygen.

141
00:14:21.440 --> 00:14:33.090
The precise rate of this reaction is still considered the most important unmeasured number in nuclear astrophysics; in a handful of accelerator laboratories around the world, including one set up underground,

142
00:14:33.090 --> 00:14:36.100
that value has been pursued for years.

143
00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:39.910
The Hoyle state itself is not a closed file either.

144
00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:44.680
The stable state of carbon-12 is a nearly spherical, tightly bound structure.

145
00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:47.370
The Hoyle state is not.

146
00:14:47.370 --> 00:14:58.380
The calculations and lattice simulations of the past two decades describe this excited state as a structure in which three alpha clusters are arranged like a loose, swollen triangle with unequal corners,

147
00:14:58.380 --> 00:15:02.670
its volume several times that of stable carbon.

148
00:15:02.670 --> 00:15:12.080
Some theorists read this as a dilute nuclear gas in which the alpha particles move almost independently; others insist on a bent triangular geometry.

149
00:15:12.080 --> 00:15:15.410
That debate continues and is nowhere near settled.

150
00:15:15.410 --> 00:15:22.900
Which is to say that no one yet knows for certain what the energy step responsible for the existence of carbon in the universe actually looks like.

151
00:15:22.900 --> 00:15:27.030
The last link is how the carbon coming off that step escapes the star.

152
00:15:27.030 --> 00:15:33.880
A star of the Sun's mass, as it begins to exhaust its helium, swells outward and becomes a red giant.

153
00:15:33.880 --> 00:15:39.770
The carbon produced in the core is carried toward the surface by deep and violent convective currents.

154
00:15:39.770 --> 00:15:47.420
The star's outer layers are now held only loosely; dense stellar winds blow this carbon-rich material slowly out into space.

155
00:15:47.420 --> 00:15:53.950
At the end of its life the star releases its outer layers entirely and leaves behind a core like an ember.

156
00:15:53.950 --> 00:16:05.600
As the ejected material cools, it forms soot and microscopic diamond-like grains; these dusts mix into interstellar clouds, where they enter the dough of new stars and new planets.

157
00:16:05.600 --> 00:16:10.370
About 99 percent of the natural carbon on Earth is carbon-12.

158
00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:21.380
Nearly all of those nuclei — in a proportion high enough to call it without exception — have passed through these three steps at the center of a star: a beryllium-8 nucleus doomed to fall apart,

159
00:16:21.380 --> 00:16:29.670
a third alpha particle arriving just in time, and an energy step waiting in that narrow interval of 287 kiloelectronvolts.

160
00:16:29.670 --> 00:16:40.360
Every single carbon atom in the graphite of your pencil, in the carbon dioxide of the air you breathe, in the cells forming this sentence, has stepped on that impossible rung and crossed.

161
00:16:40.360 --> 00:16:44.330
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

162
00:16:44.330 --> 00:16:47.500
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
